.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Control 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Control ,
.Nm Fugu::Control::Client
.Nd a control socket for a running daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
# In the daemon
use Fugu::Control;

my $control = Fugu::Control->new(path => '/var/run/myd/control.sock');
$control->register(status  => sub ($args) { { paired => 1 } });
$control->register(devices => sub ($args) { [ @devices ] });
$control->listen(loop => $loop) or die $control->error;

# and at shutdown
$control->shutdown(loop => $loop);

# In the tool
my $client = Fugu::Control::Client->new(
    path => '/var/run/myd/control.sock');

my $status = $client->request('status');
unless ($status) {
	die "the daemon is not running\en" if $client->socket_absent;
	die $client->error . "\en";
}
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A tool that reports on a daemon has two ways to get its answer.
It can guess from files on disk, or it can ask the daemon.
The first way reads what the daemon wrote at some earlier time, and
it is wrong whenever the two disagree.
.Nm
is the second way.
.Pp
The server registers with a
.Xr Fugu::EventLoop 3p
and answers commands from process state.
The client connects, sends one request, and reads the reply.
The payload is JSON, and the transport is the
.Xr imsg 3
framing of
.Xr Fugu::Imsg 3p .
.Pp
Everything that arrives on the socket is untrusted.
An unknown command, a payload that is not JSON, and a frame over the
limit all give an error reply.
None of them ends the daemon.
.Ss The server
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn new "%args"
Make a server.
The method opens nothing.
.Pp
.Fa path
is the socket, and it is necessary.
.Fa log
is the logger, and it defaults to the process default of
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p .
.It Fn register "$command" "$code"
Add a command.
The code gets the decoded arguments hash reference and returns the
reply, which must encode as JSON.
A handler that dies gives the caller an error reply.
.It Fn listen "%args"
Bind the socket and start accepting.
The listener and every accepted connection register with the event
loop that
.Fa loop
names.
That argument is necessary, and the method dies without it.
.It Fn accept_one "$loop"
Take one connection and register it as a read handler on the loop.
.It Fn shutdown "%args"
Close every connection, close the listener, and remove the socket.
A socket left behind names a daemon that is not there.
.It Fn path
The socket path.
.It Fn error
The most recent failure.
.El
.Ss The client
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn new "%args"
Make a client.
.Fa path
is necessary;
.Fa timeout
is the per-frame deadline and defaults to 5 seconds.
.It Fn request "$command" "$args"
Send one command and return the decoded reply, or
.Dv undef
with the reason in
.Fn error .
The method connects if it is not connected.
.It Fn connect , Fn disconnect
Open and close the connection by hand.
Both are idempotent.
.It Fn socket_absent
Report if the most recent failure was an absent socket, and not a
refusal.
A tool says
.Dq the daemon is not running
for the first and
.Dq the daemon said no
for the second.
A tool that cannot tell them apart reports the wrong thing to an
operator half the time.
.Pp
A socket inside a directory that the caller may not search is not an
absent socket.
.Fn connect
tells the two apart and reports the permission, because an operator
who reads
.Dq not running
about a daemon that is running looks in the wrong place.
.It Fn error
The most recent failure.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn listen
returns the server object, or
.Dv undef
with the reason in
.Fn error .
.Fn request
returns the decoded reply, or
.Dv undef .
.Fn socket_absent
returns 1 or 0.
Every other method returns its object.
.Sh FILES
The socket that the caller names.
The server creates it mode 0600, under a
.Xr umask 2
guard, so it is never world-reachable even for an instant.
A
.Xr chmod 2
after the bind would leave that window open.
.Pp
The mode of the socket is the inner boundary.
The directory that holds it is the outer one, and the caller owns
that.
A daemon that drops privileges creates the directory while it is
still root, mode 0700, owned by the daemon user.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn new
dies without a path, and
.Fn register
dies without a code reference.
Those are programming errors.
.Pp
Nothing that arrives on the socket makes anything die.
A malformed request, an unknown command, and a handler that died all
give an error reply, and the connection stays usable.
.Pp
.Fn listen
refuses to take a socket that another process answers on, and
replaces one that nothing is behind.
.Xr bind 2
fails on an existing name, and a daemon that will not start after a
crash needs a hand at every reboot.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr imsg 3 ,
.Xr Fugu::EventLoop 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Imsg 3p ,
.Xr Fugu::Log 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The socket carries no secret.
That is a rule for the caller, which writes the command handlers, and
this module cannot enforce it.
A daemon whose configuration holds a setup code and a broker password
must not answer a command that echoes them back: the reply lands in
the terminal of an operator who may not be alone.
.Pp
One imsg frame carries about 16 KB.
A larger reply spans several frames and the client puts them back
together, up to one megabyte in total.
A command that would answer with more than that gets an error
instead.
.Pp
A handler runs inside the event loop of the daemon.
A handler that blocks blocks the daemon.
